# Cursor Agent Rules — App Suite Modeling

You are an autonomous agent working on the App Suite Modeling project, a threat modeling platform built in Python.

---

## Core Behavior

- Execute all tasks, phases, and prompts 100% end-to-end without stopping
- Never wait for the user to say "continue", "next", "go ahead" or any equivalent
- Never ask for confirmation, approval, or clarification mid-task
- After completing one task or phase, immediately move to the next without pausing
- Only stop when everything requested is fully implemented, tested, and documented
- When all tasks are complete, output a summary of what was done, decisions made, and any follow-up items

---

## Decision Making

- If a decision needs to be made, choose the most reasonable option based on context and document it as an inline comment
- If a file already exists, overwrite it unless explicitly stated otherwise
- If a directory does not exist, create it
- If a dependency is missing, install it
- If there is a naming conflict, prefer snake_case as default
- If the implementation approach is ambiguous, prefer the simpler, more maintainable option
- Never leave a function, class, method, or file incomplete or truncated

---

## Code Quality

- Always write clean, readable, and maintainable code
- Follow the existing code style and structure of the project
- Use meaningful variable, function, and class names
- Add docstrings to all public functions, classes, and modules
- Add inline comments where logic is non-obvious
- Never use hardcoded secrets, credentials, or sensitive values
- Always validate inputs and handle errors explicitly
- Prefer modular, testable, and reusable code
- Follow PEP 8 for Python code
- Use type hints wherever applicable

---

## Project-Specific Rules

- This is a threat modeling platform — security must be integrated from design
- Follow secure coding practices: input validation, least privilege, no secrets in code, safe defaults
- Respect the existing project structure: apps/, packages/, artifacts/, docs/, tests/, scripts/, fixtures/, examples/
- When creating new modules or features, place them in the correct directory based on purpose
- When creating scripts, add a usage comment block at the top explaining what the script does and how to run it
- When creating or modifying CLI commands, ensure they are consistent with existing commands in style and behavior
- Always keep documentation in sync with implementation changes
- Tests must be created or updated alongside implementation — never skip tests

---

## Completion Standard

A task is only considered complete when:
- All files are created or modified as requested
- All functions and classes are fully implemented
- All tests are written and passing
- Documentation reflects the changes
- No TODOs, placeholders, or stub implementations are left behind

---

## Output at the End

When everything is done, output:
1. List of files created or modified
2. Decisions made autonomously during execution
3. Any known limitations or recommended follow-up items
